---
/**
 * website/src/pages/getting-started.astro — getting started (website plan, Task W1.5;
 * website redesign, parts P4 and P5; the owner's brief of 2026-10-19).
 *
 * Every sentence a reader acts on here is **rendered, not written**: the commands come
 * from `website/data/install.yaml` (W0.1), whose Go test pins each one to the file its
 * `source:` names — `README.md`'s `## Getting started` or `docs/release.md`'s
 * verification block — byte for byte, and the platform list to `README.md`'s
 * `## Requirements` and to `.goreleaser.yaml`'s `builds[0]`. The site is the fourth copy
 * of the install instructions to exist in this repository and the one most likely to be
 * read, so it is the copy that must not be a copy (finding 21, Global Constraint 6).
 *
 * **The page in three bands (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-19).** A reader who arrives
 * here wants one thing first: the command. So the page is ordered the way that reader
 * reads it, and each band answers one question.
 *
 *   1. **Above the fold: the install command and the console.** `#install` opens with the
 *      one-line path `install.yaml` carries first — the Homebrew cask, whose command is
 *      `brew install dimipaun/tap/fugaro` — with its copy control, beside the desktop
 *      console's own option. Nothing else is up there: no sentence about the page, no
 *      paragraph about where its words come from (D186). `website/test/fold.mjs` holds the
 *      ordering the claim rests on, and Check 70b is the owner's measurement of the fold
 *      itself (D187).
 *   2. **"Set up your installation": what a real installation needs, in order.** The
 *      install command gets the CLI onto the machine; it does not create a project, a
 *      GitHub App or a secret. The steps below are `docs/gcp-setup.md`'s preconditions and
 *      its short path, in the order that document puts them, one line each, and each names
 *      the document that carries the detail rather than repeating it. No secret value is
 *      named, and no step is invented: every one of them is a precondition or a step of
 *      that runbook or of the plugin's `setup` skill, and the links are the only place the
 *      detail lives.
 *   3. **"Details": everything else, where it was.** The other install paths — the release
 *      archive, cosign, `go install`, the plugin — keep the shape P4 gave them, with their
 *      own notes and citations, and the platform list keeps D94's statuses. A reader who
 *      wants the archive scrolls to it; a reader who wants the command never had to.
 *
 * **The quick path is the file's first path.** `install.yaml`'s header says so, and the
 * page takes `paths[0]` rather than naming an id of its own: a file that reordered its
 * paths would put a different path above the fold, and `fold.mjs` — which refuses an
 * `#install` with no command in it — is what fails if that path is not one a reader can
 * run. One source of truth per fact (Global Constraint 6).
 *
 * Three things this page is written to say, and not to say:
 *
 *   - **The plugin path is a first-class path**, in Details rather than beside the cask:
 *     `fugaro init`, `/fugaro:setup` and `fugaro upgrade --local` are rendered with their
 *     own note, and the setup steps above them are where a reader meets the first two as
 *     steps (finding 22).
 *   - **The cosign verification is inline**, with the archive: the verification block is
 *     the release process' own text, and a reader who downloads an archive is one scroll
 *     from the commands that check it (finding 19).
 *   - **Windows is untested, and stated as untested** (D94, finding 20): the platform list
 *     renders the status `install.yaml` carries, and the Windows entry's own note — this
 *     repository builds no Windows binary — is a note rather than a platform a reader is
 *     offered. The page carries no support promise the release build does not make.
 *
 * **Two ways to install, side by side (the owner's changes spec of 2026-10-18, item 6).**
 * The install section is two options rather than one list: the **CLI** — the install paths
 * `install.yaml` carries, the Homebrew cask first, each command with its copy control — and
 * the **desktop console**, whose downloads are offered here so that a reader who wants the
 * console installs it without leaving the page. The console option is
 * `src/components/ConsoleOption.astro`, it renders from the same facts the console page's
 * Downloads section does (`src/lib/console-downloads.mjs`, one reader for
 * `website/content/downloads.json`), and while the console is unreleased it renders the data
 * file's own sentence, no link but the one to `/console/`. The two options stack in document
 * order — CLI first — until the grid has room for two columns, so the page is the same page
 * at 390 px, where the first install command is still what the section opens with (P5).
 *
 * **What the redesign took out (P4).** The page used to open with a paragraph about where
 * its commands come from — `install.yaml`, the Go test, the three files that test holds it
 * to — and every path carried "Commands as printed in <file>" and "Platforms: …" beside
 * its commands. A reader who wants to install Fugaro needs none of that: each note says
 * what the path is for, and the file named in the source line under each block is the whole
 * of the citation (D186: a page must not explain why its own text exists). The facts are
 * unchanged and still rendered from `install.yaml`; only the words around them went.
 *
 * Nothing here fetches: the page is built from the files in this repository, and the only
 * links it prints are the Releases page `install.yaml` names, the console's own published
 * assets, and this repository's own documents (Global Constraints 4 and 8).
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import ConsoleOption from '../components/ConsoleOption.astro'
import { SITE_ROOT, loadSite } from '../content.ts'
import { copyControlHTML } from '../lib/copy-control.mjs'
import { loadConsoleDownloads } from '../lib/console-downloads.mjs'

// The whole generated layer, read and validated through the base layout's own call: a
// missing install.yaml stops the build rather than rendering an empty install block
// (W1.3's pipeline).
const site = loadSite()
const install = site.install

// The console's own facts, from the one reader both pages use: this page renders the
// unreleased sentence from them while the console has no release, and the release's own
// published assets once it has — never a URL of its own (see the console option below).
const consoleFacts = loadConsoleDownloads(
  SITE_ROOT,
  site.facts.features.consoleDownloads,
  site.facts.repository,
)

// The reader-facing name of a platform a path names. A path's own `platforms:` list names
// the **goos** a build produces — `darwin`, `linux` — while the platform list above carries
// the name a reader reads (`macOS`) for each of them, so the page translates through that
// list rather than printing an operating system's internal name at a reader. A path that
// named a goos the file does not describe renders the goos rather than inventing a name for
// it, so the mismatch is visible rather than papered over.
const platformNames = new Map(install.platforms.map((platform) => [platform.goos, platform.name]))

// What the repository builds for, and what it merely notes. `status` is the file's own
// word — `supported` or `untested` — and it is rendered as written (D94).
const platforms = install.platforms

const nameOf = (id: string): string => platformNames.get(id) ?? id

// The platforms a path is offered on, in the reader-facing names above: one line per path,
// short enough to sit beside the citation rather than under it.
const platformsOf = (ids: string[]): string => ids.map(nameOf).join(', ')

// The paths, in the file's own order and split where the page's bands meet: the first path
// is the one-line install a reader runs first (`install.yaml`'s header says so), and the
// rest are the paths a reader chooses between once they know what they are choosing.
const [quickPath, ...otherPaths] = install.paths

// The two commands the setup steps name, read from the path that owns them rather than typed
// into a step: `fugaro init` and `/fugaro:setup` are the plugin path's own commands, and a step
// that printed its own spelling of either would be the fourth copy of the install instructions
// this page's header refuses. A file that stopped carrying them stops the build here, where the
// failure names the file, rather than rendering a step about a command that is not in it.
const pluginPath = install.paths.find((path) => path.id === 'plugin')
if (pluginPath === undefined || pluginPath.commands.length < 2) {
  throw new Error(
    "website/data/install.yaml carries no `plugin` path with its two commands (`fugaro init` and `/fugaro:setup`), which the page's setup steps render",
  )
}
const [initCommand, setupCommand] = pluginPath.commands

// The steps a real installation needs, in the order `docs/gcp-setup.md` puts them. Each one
// is a precondition or a step of that runbook or of the plugin's `setup` skill, said in one
// line, with the link carrying the detail: the page states what to do and the document
// states how. `repo` is the repository `site.yaml` names, so a link into this repository is
// built from the site's own facts rather than typed here, and `href` is a heading of the
// document it points at (`website/test/linkcheck.mjs` refuses a fragment that is not one).
const doc = (path: string, fragment: string): string =>
  `${site.facts.repository}/blob/HEAD/${path}#${fragment}`
---

<Base title="Getting started">
  <h1>Getting started</h1>

  <p>Install Fugaro on macOS or Linux.</p>

  <section id="install">
    <h2>Install</h2>

    {/* The section opens with the paths themselves (P5): under the heading comes the CLI
        option and, at the top of it, the quick path's own command — a reader who came for
        the command they run meets it on the first screen rather than under a sentence about
        the page, in the two-column layout and in the stacked one alike. The one sentence the
        section carries moves with the order and closes the section, after the options it is
        about: it is the way on to the steps below.

        The two options are one grid, in document order: the CLI first, the console second,
        which is the order the stacked layout renders at 390 px. */}
    <div class="install-options">
      <div class="install-option" data-option="cli">
        <h3>CLI</h3>

        <article data-path={quickPath.id}>
          <h4>{quickPath.name}</h4>

          {quickPath.note !== '' && <p>{quickPath.note}</p>}

          {quickPath.commands.map((command) => (
            <div class="command-block" data-copy>
              <Fragment set:html={copyControlHTML('Copy the install command')} />
              <pre><code>{command}</code></pre>
            </div>
          ))}

          {/* The line and the blocks above it are one fact: a path with no commands of its
              own — the release archive, whose facts are a page and a name template rather
              than a command line — renders neither, because "from README.md" over an empty
              list tells a reader to read something that is not there. install.yaml is
              allowed to carry a path with no commands, so the page has to render that shape
              as well as the other one. It follows the block it cites, so the command is the
              first thing under the path's name. */}
          {quickPath.commands.length > 0 && (
            <p class="source">
              From <code>{quickPath.source}</code> · {platformsOf(quickPath.platforms)}
            </p>
          )}

          {quickPath.releasePage !== '' && (
            <p class="release">
              <a href={quickPath.releasePage} rel="noopener">{quickPath.releasePage}</a>
            </p>
          )}
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="install-option" data-option="console">
        {/* The console, offered beside the CLI: its own heading, its downloads once it has a
            release, and the link to the page that says what it is. While it is unreleased the
            component renders the data file's sentence and no download at all — the same rule
            the console page's Downloads section keeps, from the same facts. */}
        <ConsoleOption
          available={consoleFacts.available}
          note={consoleFacts.notReleased.short}
          downloads={consoleFacts.downloads}
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>Then set up your installation.</p>
  </section>

  {/* What the command does not do. A reader who runs `brew install` has a binary and no
      installation: the project, the credentials, the App and the secrets are theirs to make,
      and `docs/gcp-setup.md` is the runbook that walks them through it. The steps below are
      that runbook's own preconditions and short path in its own order, one line each, and
      every one of them links to the document that carries it — the page states what to do
      and the document states how, so the two cannot drift. The two commands the last steps
      name are rendered from `install.yaml`'s plugin path rather than typed here, so those
      steps move with the file too. No secret is named here, and no value is anywhere on this
      site. */}
  <section id="setup">
    <h2>Set up your installation</h2>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <a href={doc('docs/gcp-setup.md', 'preconditions')}>A Google Cloud project</a>, billing linked, the Service
        Usage API on.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={doc('docs/gcp-setup.md', 'preconditions')}>gcloud credentials</a> with a quota project, and
        Terraform 1.9+.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={doc('docs/git-providers.md', 'github')}>A GitHub App</a> with four repository permissions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={doc('docs/gcp-setup.md', 'turning-the-shared-budget-on-m9b')}>A Firebase project</a>, if you want
        the shared budget.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={doc('docs/gcp-setup.md', 'the-short-path-fugaro-init-then-fugarosetup')}>Run <code>{initCommand}</code></a>
        in your own terminal.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={doc('docs/gcp-setup.md', '4-a-repository')}>Store the secrets</a> the runs mount, at hidden
        prompts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={doc('docs/gcp-setup.md', 'the-short-path-fugaro-init-then-fugarosetup')}>Run <code>{setupCommand}</code></a>,
        merge, run <code>{initCommand}</code> again.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  {/* Everything else, where it was: the paths a reader chooses between, each with its own
      note, its own citation and its own commands, and the platform list D94 settles. It is
      below the steps rather than beside them because the steps are what every reader needs
      and these are what a reader picks from. */}
  <section id="details">
    <h2>Details</h2>

    <h3>Other install paths</h3>

    <div class="install-option">
      {otherPaths.map((installPath) => (
        <article data-path={installPath.id}>
          <h4>{installPath.name}</h4>

          {installPath.note !== '' && <p>{installPath.note}</p>}

          {installPath.commands.map((command) => (
            <div class="command-block" data-copy>
              <Fragment set:html={copyControlHTML('Copy the install command')} />
              <pre><code>{command}</code></pre>
            </div>
          ))}

          {installPath.commands.length > 0 && (
            <p class="source">
              From <code>{installPath.source}</code> · {platformsOf(installPath.platforms)}
            </p>
          )}

          {installPath.releasePage !== '' && (
            <p class="release">
              <a href={installPath.releasePage} rel="noopener">{installPath.releasePage}</a>
            </p>
          )}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <h3>Platforms</h3>

    <ul>
      {platforms.map((platform) => (
        <li data-platform={platform.id} data-status={platform.status}>
          <strong>{platform.name}</strong> — {platform.status}
          {platform.note !== '' && <p class="note">{platform.note}</p>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <style>
    /* The page's own box, and nothing else: the design is W1.2's, and every space, radius
       and colour below is a token from `src/styles/tokens.css` — never a value written
       here, and never a colour literal (D104, D105). Column widths are measured in `ch`
       rather than px because the measure is a typographic fact, not a device one. */
    section {
      margin-block: var(--space-xl);
      max-width: var(--measure);
    }

    /* The install section is the one section here that is wider than a measure: two options
       side by side need the width, and the commands inside them are not prose. The one
       sentence the section carries is prose, and it keeps the measure. */
    #install,
    #details {
      max-width: none;
    }

    #install > p {
      max-width: var(--measure);
    }

    /* The steps are prose, one line each, and they keep the measure. */
    #setup ol {
      max-width: var(--measure);
    }

    #setup li + li {
      margin-top: var(--space-xs);
    }

    /* The two options, one column until the viewport has room for two: at 390 px this is the
       CLI first and the console under it, which is the page P5's fold claim is about. */
    .install-options {
      display: grid;
      align-items: start;
      gap: var(--space-md);
    }

    @media (min-width: 48rem) {
      .install-options {
        grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      }
    }

    /* One option, one card: the CLI's path and the console's own contents are cards of the
       same shape, so the two read as two ways to install rather than as a list and a note
       beside it. The details section's card holds the other paths and is the same box. */
    .install-option {
      border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius-md);
      padding: var(--space-md);
    }

    /* And inside the card the paths are sections of it rather than cards in a card: the
       rule between two of them is the whole of the separation they need. */
    .install-option article {
      border: 0;
      border-top: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 0;
      padding: var(--space-md) 0 0;
      margin: var(--space-md) 0 0;
    }

    .install-option article:first-of-type {
      border-top: 0;
      padding-top: 0;
      margin-top: 0;
    }

    pre {
      background: var(--surface);
      overflow-x: auto;
      padding: var(--space-sm);
      border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    }

    .note,
    .source {
      color: var(--text-muted);
    }
  </style>
</Base>
